{{Short description|Governing doctrine of the Ikazaki Cabinet}}
The {{Nihongo|'''Fortress State'''|要塞国家|Yōsai Kokka}} is the governing doctrine of the [[Ikazaki Cabinet]] of Prime Minister [[Kousuke Ikazaki]], articulated during the [[Okaiken general election, 2026|2026 general election]] campaign and given statutory form in the government's first five months in office. As a governing philosophy it prioritises structural resilience over ideological ambition: the state must first be able to withstand shocks — blackouts, sieges, sanctions, war — before it can afford to pursue any programme at all. In domestic policy this has meant a national audit of critical infrastructure against fixed standards of autonomous operation; in foreign policy it has meant armed neutrality under the [[Ninth Amendment]], under which Okaiken pays for humanitarian rescue while refusing every commitment that would carry its forces into the [[Wolffrean War]].

The doctrine's two faces are joined by a single proposition, stated by Ikazaki during the October 2026 Diet debate: a state that will not fight the war must be able to guarantee that it cannot be dragged into it.

==Etymology==
The term pairs {{Nihongo|''yōsai''|要塞||fortress}} with {{Nihongo|''kokka''|国家||state}}. Ikazaki's own gloss, given in the leaders' debates, is that a fortress is defined by what it refuses to do: it defends what is inside its walls and does not sortie. Critics have noted that the metaphor concedes the opposition's framing — that Okaiken's foreign policy begins from the assumption of threat — while supporters cite the same quality as the doctrine's chief virtue: it is the first governing philosophy in the post-war era to define the nation's interests by its dependencies rather than its ambitions. Where [[Kuronomics]], the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] programme of the Kurosawa years, sought growth through exposure, the Fortress State seeks security through depth.

==Origins==
===The Defence Ministry years===
The doctrine draws directly on Ikazaki's record as [[Minister of Defence (Okaiken)|Minister of Defence]] from 2016 to 2021 in the Aoyagi government, where he conducted a sustained campaign against the semiconductor monopoly [[Kizan Systems]] and the algorithmic governance contractor Satori AI. Ikazaki restricted both firms' access to critical state contracts on the grounds that infrastructure too complex for the state to operate itself was infrastructure the state did not control. Successors quietly reversed the restrictions. The [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]] of 2026 vindicated the warnings in the most public way available to a catastrophe, and the Blackout became the doctrine's founding narrative: the moment the archipelago discovered that most of its critical systems could not run for three days without external digital dependency.

===The party tradition===
The Fortress State is also a settlement with the [[Sanese Communist Party]]'s own history. The SCP has opposed militarism since its founding in 1917, defended the original Article 9 of 1984, and opposed the 2014 Thirteenth Amendment that revised it; it has likewise opposed the [[Okaiken-Volisania Mutual Security Assistance Pact]] since 1952 on the ground that alliances subordinate Okaiken's security to another power's strategic interests. Ikazaki, a former Defence Minister, resolved the tension between that tradition and the demands of office with the formula that anti-militarism does not mean defencelessness: the party's commitment to peace requires the capacity to defend the nation, and nothing more than that capacity. The party's Marxist-Leninist wing regards the synthesis as a betrayal of pacifist principle; the electorate, in the judgment of every post-election analysis, regarded it as the first left-wing security platform it had been offered that did not ask it to choose between its conscience and its safety.

==Principles==
The doctrine is conventionally summarised in five propositions.

'''Structural resilience precedes programme.''' No policy ambition is worth more than the state's ability to survive its failure. Government's first duty is continuity: currency, defence, rail, energy and communications must function under stress before any of them may be modernised, privatised, or optimised.

'''Ninety days.''' The "Fortress Okaiken" standard requires that all critical state infrastructure — currency, defence, rail, energy — be able to function for a minimum of ninety days without external digital dependency. The figure is not arbitrary: it is the length of the Satori Blackout's worst-case scenario as modelled by the Defence Ministry's own contingency staff in 2019, a document Ikazaki has cited in every major speech on the subject since.

'''The Blackout Standard.''' Beneath the ninety-day ceiling sits a floor: every critical national system must demonstrate seventy-two hours of autonomous operation under audit. The National Resilience Framework established the review; the [[Digital Sovereignty Act]] gave it the force of law.

'''No contractor is too critical to prosecute.''' Institutional accountability is a resilience measure, not a moral posture: a state dependent on falsified compliance documentation is a state with undocumented failure points. The [[IAA Commission]] and the prosecution of Kizan Systems are the doctrine's legal teeth.

'''Shields, not invasion manifests.''' Externally, the doctrine holds that the [[Ninth Amendment]] is a complete security policy. Okaiken's forces defend the archipelago and protect Sanese lives and property wherever found; they join no alliances, serve under no foreign command, and fight no wars that are not Okaiken's own. Humanitarian action is unlimited; belligerency is forbidden.

==Statutory expression==
The doctrine's first legislative programme arrived within weeks of the cabinet's formation. The Digital Sovereignty Act, the SDP's flagship bill steered through the Diet by Digital Sovereignty Minister Shizuka Ono, received Imperial assent on 8 June 2026: a 40 per cent cap on any single AI system's share of an essential service grid, mandatory analogue backup for critical infrastructure, and the 72-hour Blackout Standard. The National Resilience Framework established the audit machinery that measures the rest of the state against the ninety-day ceiling. The Institutional Accountability Act followed on 17 June, creating the IAA Commission with authority to investigate and refer for prosecution any private contractor found to have falsified compliance documentation.

The October Maritime Security and Non-Belligerence Legislative Package of 23–24 October 2026 — including the [[Emergency Maritime Civilian Protection Act]] — extended the doctrine to the sea. The government described the package, in the phrase now standard in editorial commentary, as the Ninth Amendment and the Fortress State doctrine given statutory form: defensive rules of engagement for the [[Escort Flotilla]], a statutory ban on ground combat involvement, and parliamentary ratification of Okaiken's diplomatic promotion of [[Adonian Community Resolution 4942D|Resolution 4942D]], which would codify the evacuation corridors into Assembly law rather than confidential cable traffic.

==The Fortress State abroad==
The doctrine received its first wartime test in the [[Wolffrean War]]. The Escort Flotilla — two destroyers including the ''ISN Shirakumo'' and a replenishment vessel — deployed to [[Alwana]] in September 2026 under the defensive provisions of the Ninth Amendment and the humanitarian umbrella of [[Adonian Community Resolution 4935|Resolution 4935]], escorting eleven Sanese-flagged ferries chartered by the [[Adonian Community]] for the evacuation of civilians. Okaiken's Assembly votes under Foreign Minister [[Kenichi Mori]] — in favour of the rescue machinery, abstaining on or opposing every escalation — were read abroad as the doctrine's arithmetic made visible.

The demand from three directions that the flotilla be placed under Community or coalition command — the [[Burawa]]n objection through the [[JWG-PNK]], the [[Jarin]] amendment, and the [[Zong]] demarche — was refused in the government's formal response of 25 October 2026, delivered in Mori's name and reported as the doctrine's fullest public formulation. Invoking the 925 Sanese personnel who served under a Community command in the [[Adonian Community Mission for Lacashe|Lacashe mission]], the statement declared Okaiken "bound by the treaties it has signed, and by nothing else". Ikazaki's own defence of the doctrine in the Diet debate of 23–24 October has become its most-quoted text: "We will not avenge three wounded sailors by offering up three thousand more to an imperialist quagmire. This cabinet will arm our ships with shields, not with invasion manifests."

Editorialists described the emergency resolution of 24 October as the Fortress State doctrine applied to the sea. In [[Areza]], diplomatic reporting read the sovereignty framing as addressed less to the coalition than to the Secretariat itself.

==Reception and criticism==
Within the SCP, the doctrine remains contested. The Marxist-Leninist wing regards the entire edifice as a betrayal of the party's pacifist principles — a criticism that predates the premiership and that the 2014 referendum result, which overrode the party's opposition to the Thirteenth Amendment, has never fully silenced. The reformist leadership's answer is electoral: the Fortress State is the first SCP security platform to win a general election.

The opposition attacks the doctrine from both flanks. The LDP argues that the Ninth Amendment does not require passivity — that a fortress that never fires invites siege — and pressed throughout October 2026 for rules of engagement that would permit the suppression of shore batteries beyond the intercept-and-silence standard. The [[Okaiken Innovation Party]] argued the opposite: that the doctrine's logic required recalling the flotilla entirely rather than defending it. The PPO's Ryota Endo characterised the government's Assembly record as "a foreign policy of gestures". The parliamentary audit of Ikazaki's defence record, ongoing since the summer, is regarded by all sides as a test of the doctrine's factual foundations as much as of the Prime Minister's conduct.

Abroad, the doctrine's reception divides by circumstance. States commanding coalitions call sovereign national command a hazard; states contributing to them have cited the Lacashe precedent in Okaiken's support; and the Secretariat, in the judgment of most diplomatic reporting, has taken the point of the 25 October statement personally.

